our silver medal winner is yet a second  accolade for stanford university  made there by director anthony weeks his  film imaginary circumstances follows  three actors each with different  disabilities as they address the reality  of how hollywood tends to portray and  betray the truth when dealing with  people who are disabled  i'm still stunned i'm still stunned to  be here and  thrilled and honored and humbled and  grateful so um thank you first to the  academy for recognizing my film  and also  just for recognizing that uh this is an  issue that perhaps we could start  talking about a little bit more  i also want to give a special  congratulations to not only the other  filmmakers in the documentary category  but to all the filmmakers because this  week you've truly been an inspiration  and  i just i look forward to seeing more of  your work i look forward to  knowing you collaborating with you  perhaps  i'd like to  give a shout out to two of my alma  maters to stanford university to  the mfa program and documentary  specifically to jan krawitz chris  samuelson johnny simons and jamie  meltzer  also i did a little one-year program at  the new school and  deirdre boyle and annie howell were both  people who guided me and helped me a lot  i can't  i can't  thank my family enough because i think  that my family has been  supportive in everything that i've done  and my mom is here tonight and i'm  thrilled about that so thank you mom and  thanks to my family and thanks to olivia  for her inspiration  i'd also like to say thank you for to  support and friendship and love  and  finally i'd like to thank the three main  characters in the film because again in  terms of inspiration they have taught me  more in two years than i've learned in a  lifetime so  diana elizabeth jordan who's the main  character in the film is here tonight so  diana can you stand up please  and also i couldn't have made the film  without the support and encouragement  from robert david hall and from jerry  jewell  and from all the talented people at  performing arts studio west  finally i just have to say that  the most common refrain i've heard this  week when i've talked to people about  the film is  i've never thought of this before  and i think that  it's great because  now people are thinking of it and so the  film has done its job but  i guess it's my great hope that with  this film it will be a small  contribution to an ongoing conversation  that will transform this epiphany of i  haven't thought of this before into  meaningful complicated  representations of people with  disability on screen thank you
 

